  "query_preview": "..\n/Issued 133 PM MST Mon Nov 16 2026/ \n\n - Meteorological Overview:\n\nThis afternoon there is an upper-level ridge over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana with strong southerly flow aloft. \nThis afternoon it will remain warm (well-above seasonal averages) \nand mostly dry. This evening into Tuesday an upper-level trough \nassociated with a Pacific cold front will move over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will bring cooler \ntemperatures and widespread precipitation to the area on Tuesday. On \nTuesday a frontogenetic band of precipitation will move through \nNorth-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. This band will \ncause there to be a brief period of heavy precipitation across the \narea as it moves from west to east. Across lower-elevations of North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana when this band moves \nthrough it will bring a rapid change in precipitation type from rain \nto snow. See the Forecast Confidence & Scenarios Section for \ndetails. \n\nOn Wednesday the upper-level trough will remain over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow cold (well-below \nseasonal averages) temperatures to linger across the area. On \nWednesday morning snow will gradually diminish in a west to east \nfashion across North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana. On \nThursday an upper-level ridge briefly moves over North-central, \nCentral, and Southwestern Montana. This will allow it to be cold and \ndry across the area on Thursday. Friday through Saturday an upper-\nlevel trough moves over North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. This will bring another round of precipitation with cold \ntemperatures remaining across the area. On Sunday an upper-level \nridge will move back over North-central, Central, and Southwestern \nMontana. This will allow temperatures to warm up some but remain \nbelow seasonal averages with mostly dry weather across the area. \n-IG\n\n - Forecast Confidence & Scenarios:\n\nFrom tonight through Wednesday morning there remains uncertainty in \nwhere frontogenetical bands setup and progress and how fast snow-\nlevels and temperatures drop at lower-elevations. In any \nlocations where frontogenetical bands setup there will be a period\nof heavy snowfall with snowfall rates of one in/hour or greater. \nHowever, due NBM-warm bias from the previous days, hourly \ntemperatures were decreased Tuesday evening through Wednesday \nmorning to account for diabatic cooling due to cold air aloft and \nrapid cooling once snow begins to fall. Across lower-elevations of\nNorth-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana ground \ntemperatures are near 40 degrees. As a result it is uncertain in \nhow long on Tuesday it will take for the ground to cool down \nenough to allow snow to accumulate on grassy surfaces and road \nsurfaces across the lower-elevations of North-central, Central, \nand Southwestern Montana. \n\nFrom tonight through Wednesday morning there is a 40 - 80% chance \nfor 6 inches of snow or greater in the Madison and Gallatin \nMountains and along the Northern Rocky Mountain Front. From tonight \nthrough Wednesday morning there is a 40 - 80% chance for 4 inches of \nsnow or greater across the Mountains of North-central, Central, and \nSouthwestern Montana. Across most of the lower-elevations of North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana there is a 30 - 60% \nchance for 1 inch of snow or greater tonight through Wednesday \nmorning. However, snowfall probabilities across the lower-elevations \nof North-central, Central, and Southwestern Montana remain too low \ndue to a warm bias in the NBM. As a result across all of North-\ncentral, Central, and Southwestern Montana Winter Weather \nAdvisories are in effect for Tuesday through Wednesday. See the\nTFX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES Section for details. Across \nCentral and Southwestern Montana and North-central Montana along \nand west of I-15 there is a 60 - 85% chance for one half inch of \ntotal liquid precipitation tonight through Wednesday morning. -IG",
